 { Black’s counterplay against the d4-pawn The position in the diagram occurred in the game Getz – Istratescu, Oslo 2013. Black began to obtain counterplay with: } 13... Ne4 $1 { The first part of the plan; this knight disrupts White’s coordination. } 14. Bb2 $2 ( { But not: } 14. Nxe4 $2 dxe4 15. Bxd7 ( { or } 15. Ne5 Bxb5 16. Qxb5 Qxd4 $17 ) 
exf3 16. Qc4 Qxd7 17. Qxb4 fxg2 $17 ) 
 ( { PH: The computer immediately suggests the inhuman } 14. Bxd7 $1 { when } Nxc3 { is the move that should be checked of course. } 15. Qd2 Nbxa2 { and now } 16. Ba4 $1 { when Black's best seems to be } Nxc1 17. Rfxc1 Nxa4 18. Rxa4 $14 { White is a pawn down, but the c5-pawn is a big asset and Black is likely to lose their a-pawn in the near future. } ) 
 ( { PH: Another decent way of continuing is } 14. Bd2 $5 { . Compared to the game, after } Bxb5 15. Nxb5 Nc6 16. Rab1 $11 { obviously White's bishop is better placed compared to the game where it was on b2. } ) 
Bxb5 15. Nxb5 Nc6 { The second part in Black’s plan, pressuring the d4-pawn. } 16. Nc3 f5 $1 { Now the third part, supporting the knight on e4 and preparing the last part of the plan: ...Bf6 with substantial pressure on d4. } ( { PH: } 16... Ng5 $5 { ⩱ is also strong, trying to weaken the d4-pawn further: } 17. Nxg5 Bxg5 18. Rfd1 Bf6 { and the position plays itself for Black. } ) 
17. Nxe4 ( { If } 17. Rab1 Bf6 18. Rfd1 Qd7 19. Ba1 Rab8 { and Black is at least equal. } ) 
dxe4 ( 17... fxe4 $5 { was promising too, for example } 18. Nd2 Rb8 19. Bc3 e5 $5 20. Nb3 exd4 21. Nxd4 Qd7 $15 { when the c5-pawn is very weak. } ) 
18. Qc4 ( { Or: } 18. Rfd1 Qd5 $1 ( { but not } 18. Qc4 exf3 $2 19. Qxe6 Kh8 20. Qxc6 $16 ) 
19. Ne5 Nxe5 20. dxe5 Qxc5 $15 ) 
Qd5 $1 19. Qxd5 exd5 20. Ne1 Rab8 21. Bc3 Bf6 22. Nc2 f4 $15 { Black had a very promising position and eventually won the game. } 
