 { Black’s ...b7-b6 counterplay The position in the diagram occurred in the game Klundt – Dautov, Bad Wiessee 2001. In order to avoid the long-term problems Black faced in Dreev – Galic, Black modified the structure immediately with: } 12... Nxc3 ( { PH: It's not possible to maintain the knight on e4 here: } 12... f5 $2 13. a3 { and it won't be possible to stop b4 next. } ) 
13. bxc3 h6 14. O-O b6 $1 { This is the key; now Black is rid of his backward b7-pawn and forces White to have a backward c3-pawn. } 15. Rfb1 Ba6 { If light-squared bishops are off the board, then even better! } ( { But not } 15... bxc5 $6 16. Rb5 Qc7 17. Rxc5 Qd6 18. Rb1 { as White is a little better coordinated. } ) 
16. Bxa6 Qxa6 17. cxb6 $6 { Imprecise. } ( { Better was } 17. Ne5 $1 Rfc8 ( { or } 17. cxb6 bxc5 $5 18. Nd7 cxd4 19. Nxf8 d3 20. Qd2 Rxf8 $13 ) 
18. Nd3 $11 { when it is hard for Black to pressure the c3-pawn. } ) 
axb6 18. Qb3 Rfc8 $15 { Black is better as both the c3- and a2-pawns are clear targets. This kind of position is exactly what Black is looking for when he plays the ...b7-b6 break. White’s once-powerful queenside majority is now just a weakness. } 
 *

